---
// ═══ THE PUBLISH, DISCARD AND ERROR OVERLAYS ════════════════════════════════
//
// SIX ARTEFACTS PLUS ONE STRIP VARIANT, ONE ROUTE EACH:
//   /admin/overlays/publish-valid/     O-publish-valid     (D-14)
//   /admin/overlays/publish-invalid/   O-publish-invalid   (D-18, G-6)
//   /admin/overlays/error-publish/     T-error-publish     (D-18, inline half)
//   /admin/overlays/error-network/     T-error-network     (D-19, cross-check)
//   /admin/overlays/discard-screen/    O-discard-screen    (D-17)
//   /admin/overlays/discard-all/       O-discard-all       (D-17)
//   /admin/overlays/pipeline-failure/  O-pipeline-strip    (D-15, failure)
// and /admin/overlays/ is the index over all seven.
//
// ═══ THE AXIS IS A REST PARAM, NOT `?o=` — A CORRECTION TO THIS PLAN ════════
// The plan specifies `?o=`. That cannot work, and it is the same correction
// plans 13, 14 and 15 each made for `?state=`: under `output: 'static'` Astro
// strips the query string in the DEV SERVER as well as at build
// (astro/dist/core/request.js:20 `if (isPrerendered) url.search = "";`, with
// vite-plugin-app/app.js:178 marking every prerendered route). `Astro.url
// .searchParams` is unconditionally empty here, so all seven overlays would
// have rendered as one page. The overlay id is a route segment instead, which
// is also what plan 17 needs: a real crawlable URL per screenshot.
//
// ═══ EVERY OVERLAY IN THE DESIGN SYSTEM SERVER-RENDERS TO NOTHING ══════════
// Re-measured with react-dom/server against the installed 1.11.4 tarball before
// this file was written, because this plan needs FOUR of them:
//
//   ConfirmDialog(open)     →     0 B
//   TypeToConfirm(open)     →     0 B
//   Sheet(open)             →     0 B
//   Modal(open)             →     0 B
//   FormErrorSummary        →   156 B   ← the one that does render
//   AlertBanner(open)       →   279 B   ← and the one D-19 leans on
//
// All four dialogs return `<DSPortal>…</DSPortal>`, and DSPortal returns null
// until a mount EFFECT has run (dist/index.js:2429-2436). Under SSR no effect
// runs. UI-SPEC's hydration table prescribes that dialogs and sheets are
// sketched "open and static" — with the components as shipped that instruction
// is not executable, and the hydration budget was fully spent by plan 14.
//
// So the chrome below is hand-authored against the design system's OWN class
// names from modal.css — ds-atom-modal-backdrop, ds-atom-modal,
// ds-atom-modal-hd, ds-atom-modal-hd-title, ds-atom-modal-body,
// ds-atom-modal-ft. Every painted pixel is still modal.css's; the only local
// thing is the mounting. Each site names the component it stands in for. It is
// the sanctioned local-prototype shape plan 15 used twice and plan 13 set the
// precedent for with density-compact.css, and it dies with this directory.
//
// TWO COMPONENTS ARE COMPOSED FOR REAL, and that is the point of measuring
// first rather than assuming: `FormErrorSummary` and `AlertBanner` both render
// under SSR, so O-publish-invalid uses the real FormErrorSummary and the D-19
// treatment uses the real AlertBanner. Only the four that render nothing are
// prototyped.
//
// ═══ THE COUNTS AGREE WITH THE DASHBOARD, AND THE BUILD PROVES IT ══════════
// D-15's "two places that must agree" is a rule about the pipeline strip, but
// the failure it names is general: a publish modal that summarises a different
// set from the one the dashboard lists is the same defect. So this file imports
// BOTH fixtures and throws if the entity set or any per-entity change count has
// drifted. Agreement is a build failure here rather than a promise.
//
// ═══ ZERO HYDRATION ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// This plan takes none of the budget. The acceptance criterion counts the
// directive prefix and requires zero, so the prefix is deliberately not spelled
// anywhere in this file — not even in prose. check-no-js.sh is the assertion
// with teeth, because it reads the emitted HTML rather than the source.
//
// ═══ SCOPE FENCE (D-02) ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// No auth, no D1, no endpoint, no publish action that does anything. Every
// button on every route in this file is inert and every number is read from a
// fixture. Nothing here posts, fetches or stores.

import {
	AlertBanner,
	Badge,
	Button,
	Card,
	Divider,
	Eyebrow,
	FormErrorSummary,
	Heading,
	Link,
	Text,
} from "@akhil-saxena/design-system";
import Admin from "../../../layouts/Admin.astro";
import fx from "../../../fixtures/overlays.json";
import dashboard from "../../../fixtures/dashboard.json";

// ── THE ARTEFACT REGISTER FOR THIS FILE ───────────────────────────────────
// Exported so the coverage generator can see which artefacts are DESIGNED on a
// route that has no coverage cell of its own. `O-` and `T-` artefacts are
// cross-cutting: they are pointed at by cells rather than owning one, which is
// exactly how `T-error-publish` came to be inherited by the dashboard while
// being designed nowhere. src/lib/coverage.mjs reads this export, unions it
// with every `designed` cell, and check-coverage.mjs fails on an `inherits` ref
// that resolves to nothing. Same shape as conflict-diff.astro's ARTEFACT.
export const ARTEFACTS = [
	{
		id: "O-publish-valid",
		route: "/admin/overlays/publish-valid",
		proves:
			"That D-14's confirmation is against a LIST of what will ship rather than a bare question, and that D-12's asynchrony for photos is stated where the decision is made rather than discovered afterwards.",
	},
	{
		id: "O-publish-invalid",
		route: "/admin/overlays/publish-invalid",
		proves:
			"That the strict half of D-18 is a designed screen naming three specific failures — and that G-6 is real: FormErrorSummary takes `errors: string[]`, so every deep link had to be rendered as a separate element beside it.",
	},
	{
		id: "T-error-publish",
		route: "/admin/overlays/error-publish",
		proves:
			"The inline publish-block treatment the dashboard's `error` cell inherits. Before this route that ref resolved to nothing — a covered-looking cell that was not covered.",
	},
	{
		id: "O-discard-screen",
		route: "/admin/overlays/discard-screen",
		proves:
			"That a per-screen discard states its own cost in the fixture's real numbers, and that D-11 is what makes the copy true: a draft is pending changes layered on published JSON, so discard always returns to published state.",
	},
	{
		id: "O-discard-all",
		route: "/admin/overlays/discard-all",
		proves:
			"That the guard scales with what it destroys — a confirm for one screen, a typed word for everything — and that the asymmetry between the two is legible when they are read one after the other.",
	},
];

// The seven overlay routes. `o: undefined` produces `/admin/overlays/`.
export const OVERLAY_IDS = [
	"publish-valid",
	"publish-invalid",
	"error-publish",
	"error-network",
	"discard-screen",
	"discard-all",
	"pipeline-failure",
];

// Astro hoists imports and TOP-LEVEL EXPORTS into module scope and leaves plain
// `const`s inside the component factory, so anything getStaticPaths reads must
// be exported. Plan 15 lost a build to exactly this.
export function getStaticPaths() {
	return [{ params: { o: undefined } }, ...OVERLAY_IDS.map((o) => ({ params: { o } }))];
}

const o = Astro.params.o ?? "index";
if (o !== "index" && !OVERLAY_IDS.includes(o)) {
	throw new Error(
		`admin/overlays/[...o]: unknown overlay "${o}". getStaticPaths emits \`undefined\` plus ` +
			"OVERLAY_IDS, so this can only be reached by editing one and not the other.",
	);
}

// ══ THE AGREEMENT GUARD ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Fail loud, before anything renders. The failure this catches is a sketch that
// looks fine on its own page and contradicts the screen it summarises.
const dashScreens = dashboard.dirty.groups;
const ourScreens = fx.session.screens;
const dashKey = dashScreens.map((g) => `${g.id}:${g.changes.length}`).join(" ");
const ourKey = ourScreens.map((g) => `${g.id}:${g.changes.length}`).join(" ");
if (dashKey !== ourKey) {
	throw new Error(
		"admin/overlays: the overlay fixture has drifted from the dashboard fixture.\n" +
			`  dashboard.json dirty : ${dashKey}\n` +
			`  overlays.json session: ${ourKey}\n` +
			"A publish modal that summarises a different set from the one the dashboard lists is " +
			"the same defect D-15 names for the pipeline strip — two places that must agree. Fix " +
			"the fixture; do not relax this check.",
	);
}

// ── EVERY NUMBER IN EVERY PIECE OF COPY BELOW IS COMPUTED, NEVER TYPED ─────
const screens = ourScreens;
const screenCount = screens.length;
const changeCount = screens.reduce((n, s) => n + s.changes.length, 0);
const fileCount = new Set(screens.map((s) => s.file)).size;
const target = screens.find((s) => s.id === fx.discardScreen.target);
const targetCount = target.changes.length;

// UI-SPEC's contract table, VERBATIM, as literal constants — so the exact
// strings are greppable in one place and cannot drift as the markup moves
// around them. This is plan 12's idiom and it is deliberately not "read the
// string out of the fixture": a contract string belongs beside the screen that
// promises it, and a fixture is data.
const PUBLISH_CTA = "Publish changes";
const KEEP_EDITING = "Keep editing";
const DISCARD_SCREEN_CTA = "Discard changes";
const NET_TITLE = "Your session expired.";
const NET_BODY = "Sign in again — nothing on this screen has been lost.";
const NET_CTA = "Sign in";
const PIPE_TITLE = "Processing failed for 2 photos.";
const PIPE_BODY = "The originals are still staged. Retrying does not re-upload them.";
const PIPE_CTA = "Retry processing";
const INVALID_TITLE = "3 things must be fixed before publishing.";

// ── THE LITERALS ARE TIED BACK TO THE DATA, SO A NUMBER CANNOT BE TYPED ───
// Two of the contract strings carry a COUNT inside them, and a count written
// into prose is a count nobody checks. Both are asserted against the fixture
// they describe, and both throw rather than warn: `Processing failed for 2
// photos.` must agree with the number of failed photos, and `3 things must be
// fixed…` with the length of the error list the same screen renders.
const CONTRACT_CHECKS = [
	[INVALID_TITLE, `${fx.invalid.errors.length} things must be fixed before publishing.`, "invalid"],
	[PIPE_TITLE, `Processing failed for ${fx.pipelineFailure.failed} photos.`, "pipelineFailure"],
	[PIPE_BODY, fx.pipelineFailure.body, "pipelineFailure.body"],
	[PIPE_CTA, fx.pipelineFailure.cta, "pipelineFailure.cta"],
	[NET_TITLE, fx.network.title, "network.title"],
	[NET_BODY, fx.network.body, "network.body"],
	[NET_CTA, fx.network.cta, "network.cta"],
	[KEEP_EDITING, fx.discardScreen.cancelLabel, "discardScreen.cancelLabel"],
	[DISCARD_SCREEN_CTA, fx.discardScreen.confirmLabel, "discardScreen.confirmLabel"],
];
for (const [literal, derived, where] of CONTRACT_CHECKS) {
	if (literal !== derived) {
		throw new Error(
			`admin/overlays: the contract string for "${where}" no longer matches the fixture.\n` +
				`  written on the screen : ${JSON.stringify(literal)}\n` +
				`  implied by the data   : ${JSON.stringify(derived)}\n` +
				"A count stated in destructive or blocking copy has to be the real count — a dialog " +
				"whose job is to state a cost may not state it from an example.",
		);
	}
}

// The two destructive strings. The `across N screens` clause is UI-SPEC's,
// verbatim and true; the counts are the fixture's — see overlays.json's README
// for why an illustrative number may not be quoted by a dialog whose job is to
// state a blast radius.
const DISCARD_SCREEN_TITLE = `Discard changes to ${target.entity}?`;
const DISCARD_SCREEN_BODY = `The ${targetCount} pending changes on this screen go back to what's published. This can't be undone.`;
const DISCARD_ALL_TITLE = "Discard all pending changes?";
const DISCARD_ALL_BODY = `${changeCount} pending changes across ${screenCount} screens go back to what's published.`;

const nav = { home: "draft", photos: "draft", resume: "ready", site: "ready" };

const FAILURE_STRIP = {
	tone: "danger",
	title: PIPE_TITLE,
	description: PIPE_BODY,
	value: 1,
	max: 3,
	label: `1 of 3 processed · ${fx.pipelineFailure.failed} failed`,
};

const INDEX = [
	{ o: "publish-valid", id: "O-publish-valid", d: "D-14", what: "Publish confirm, valid" },
	{ o: "publish-invalid", id: "O-publish-invalid", d: "D-18", what: "Publish confirm, invalid" },
	{ o: "error-publish", id: "T-error-publish", d: "D-18", what: "Publish block, inline" },
	{ o: "error-network", id: "T-error-network", d: "D-19", what: "Network / 401, inline" },
	{ o: "discard-screen", id: "O-discard-screen", d: "D-17", what: "Discard this screen" },
	{ o: "discard-all", id: "O-discard-all", d: "D-17", what: "Discard all" },
	{ o: "pipeline-failure", id: "O-pipeline-strip", d: "D-15", what: "Pipeline failure + retry" },
];

const TITLES = {
	index: "Publish, discard and error overlays",
	"publish-valid": "O-publish-valid",
	"publish-invalid": "O-publish-invalid",
	"error-publish": "T-error-publish",
	"error-network": "T-error-network",
	"discard-screen": "O-discard-screen",
	"discard-all": "O-discard-all",
	"pipeline-failure": "O-pipeline-strip — failure",
};
---

<Admin
	title={TITLES[o]}
	active="dashboard"
	route="/admin"
	navStates={nav}
	pipeline={o === "pipeline-failure" ? FAILURE_STRIP : undefined}
>
	{/* ══ THE INDEX ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */}
	{o === "index" && (
		<div class="ov-head">
			<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">CROSS-CUTTING OVERLAYS</Eyebrow>
			<Heading level={2} size="lg" weight="bold">
				Seven surfaces that belong to publishing rather than to a screen.
			</Heading>
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				None of these owns a coverage cell. They are what cells point AT — which is why the
				coverage gate now checks that an <code class="ov-code">inherits</code> reference
				resolves to an artefact somebody actually built, and not merely to a canonical id.
			</Text>
			<ol class="ov-index">
				{INDEX.map((row) => (
					<li class="ov-index-row">
						<a class="ov-index-link" href={`/admin/overlays/${row.o}/`}>{row.id}</a>
						<span class="ov-index-what">{row.what}</span>
						<Badge tone="neutral">{row.d}</Badge>
					</li>
				))}
			</ol>
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				This session is the dashboard's <code class="ov-code">dirty</code> state:
				{" "}{changeCount} changes across {screenCount} screens, in {fileCount} files. Every
				count in every dialog below is computed from it, and the build fails if the two
				fixtures diverge.
			</Text>
		</div>
	)}

	{/* ══ O-publish-valid — D-14 ════════════════════════════════════════════
	    D-14 requires explicit confirmation AGAINST A LIST of what will ship,
	    not a bare "Publish?". So the panel is a list first and a question
	    second: four entities, their files, and every individual change at its
	    real authored length.

	    IT ALSO CARRIES D-12's CONSEQUENCE, because this is where the operator
	    forms an expectation about what happens next. Publish is ASYNCHRONOUS
	    FOR PHOTOS: the binary is staged to R2, publish fires the dispatch, and
	    Actions resizes and reads EXIF afterwards. A confirm that implies the
	    gallery is updated when the commit lands teaches the wrong model, and
	    the operator then reads the pipeline strip as a fault.

	    DS composition per UI-SPEC: `ConfirmDialog tone="neutral"`. Measured at
	    0 B under SSR, so the panel is modal.css's classes with the mounting
	    local. `ConfirmDialog`'s panel additionally has NO STYLESHEET AT ALL —
	    there is no .ds-atom-confirm-panel rule anywhere under dist/css/, only
	    one inline style object with a hardcoded near-white background (plan
	    15's F-15-3) — so even a hydrated version of this dialog would not be
	    reachable from the charcoal cascade. Using modal.css here is therefore
	    the MORE faithful choice, not merely the available one. */}
	{o === "publish-valid" && (
		<div class="ds-atom-modal-backdrop ov-backdrop" data-artefact="O-publish-valid">
			<div
				class="ds-atom-modal ov-panel"
				role="dialog"
				aria-modal="true"
				aria-labelledby="pv-title"
				data-ds-component="ConfirmDialog"
				data-ds-tone="neutral"
			>
				<header class="ds-atom-modal-hd">
					<span id="pv-title" class="ds-atom-modal-hd-title">
						Publish {changeCount} changes across {screenCount} screens?
					</span>
				</header>
				<div class="ds-atom-modal-body">
					<p class="ov-lede">
						This commits {fileCount} files. Everything listed below goes live; nothing else
						changes.
					</p>
					<ul class="ov-groups">
						{screens.map((s) => (
							<li class="ov-group">
								<div class="ov-group-head">
									<span class="ov-group-entity">{s.entity}</span>
									<Badge tone={s.state === "ready" ? "info" : "warning"} dot>
										{s.state === "ready" ? "Ready" : "Draft"}
									</Badge>
									<code class="ov-code">{s.file}</code>
									<span class="ov-group-n">
										{s.changes.length} {s.changes.length === 1 ? "change" : "changes"}
									</span>
								</div>
								<ul class="ov-changes">
									{s.changes.map((c) => (
										<li>{c}</li>
									))}
								</ul>
							</li>
						))}
					</ul>

					{/* D-12, ON THE SCREEN RATHER THAN ONLY IN A DOCUMENT. */}
					<div class="ov-async">
						<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">WHAT PUBLISHING DOES NOT DO</Eyebrow>
						<p>{fx.asyncPhotos.note}</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<footer class="ds-atom-modal-ft">
					<Button variant="secondary">{KEEP_EDITING}</Button>
					<Button variant="primary">{PUBLISH_CTA}</Button>
				</footer>
			</div>
		</div>
	)}

	{/* ══ O-publish-invalid — D-18's STRICT HALF, AND G-6's EVIDENCE ════════
	    D-18 is one schema at two severities. The lenient half is T-error-inline
	    on /admin/photos/error/ — a warning that lets the draft save anyway. This
	    is the strict half: the same schema run server-side at publish time,
	    blocking, and naming every failure.

	    THE INVALID STATE IS A DESIGNED SCREEN. D-18 says so in as many words,
	    and the reason is operational rather than aesthetic: a publish button
	    that is simply switched off tells the operator nothing about what to fix,
	    so they go looking. Three named failures, each pointing at the screen
	    that owns it, is the whole difference.

	    ═══ G-6 IS VISIBLE HERE, NOT DESCRIBED ═══════════════════════════════
	    `FormErrorSummary` is composed for real — it renders 156 B under SSR, so
	    unlike the four dialogs it needed no prototype. Its prop surface is
	    exactly

	        interface FormErrorSummaryProps {
	          errors: string[];        // dist/index.d.ts:3350-3354
	          title?: string;
	          className?: string;
	        }

	    `errors` is `string[]`. There is NO href, no ReactNode, no per-error
	    action — so D-18's "deep-links to the offending screen" cannot be
	    expressed through the component at any level of effort. The three
	    `Go to …` actions below therefore render as SEPARATE ELEMENTS BESIDE the
	    summary, in the same order as the errors inside it, which is the closest
	    a consumer can get and is visibly not the same thing: the link is next to
	    the list rather than on the failure. Renumber or reorder the array and the
	    two fall out of step with nothing to catch it.

	    The component is NOT forked to add an href. That is the Core Value: the
	    gap is a finding, and this route is its evidence. */}
	{o === "publish-invalid" && (
		<div class="ds-atom-modal-backdrop ov-backdrop" data-artefact="O-publish-invalid">
			<div
				class="ds-atom-modal ov-panel"
				role="dialog"
				aria-modal="true"
				aria-labelledby="pi-title"
				data-ds-component="Modal"
			>
				<header class="ds-atom-modal-hd">
					<span id="pi-title" class="ds-atom-modal-hd-title">{INVALID_TITLE}</span>
				</header>
				<div class="ds-atom-modal-body">
					{/* THE REAL COMPONENT. */}
					<FormErrorSummary title={INVALID_TITLE} errors={fx.invalid.errors} />

					{/* G-6 — THE DEEP LINKS, BESIDE THE SUMMARY BECAUSE THEY CANNOT BE
					    INSIDE IT. `errors: string[]` carries no anchor. */}
					<div class="ov-targets" role="group" aria-label="Go to the screen that owns each failure">
						<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">
							G-6 — THESE BELONG INSIDE THE LIST ABOVE AND CANNOT BE
						</Eyebrow>
						<ol class="ov-target-list">
							{fx.invalid.targets.map((t, i) => (
								<li class="ov-target">
									<span class="ov-target-n">{i + 1}</span>
									<Link href={t.href} variant="inline">{t.label}</Link>
								</li>
							))}
						</ol>
						<p class="ov-note">
							The numbers are the only thing binding an action to its failure. The component
							takes plain strings, so the ordering is a convention two arrays happen to share
							rather than a relationship anything checks. The proposed fix is
							<code class="ov-code">errors: Array&lt;&#123; message: string; href?: string &#125;&gt;</code>.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<footer class="ds-atom-modal-ft">
					<Button variant="secondary">{KEEP_EDITING}</Button>
				</footer>
			</div>
		</div>
	)}

	{/* ══ T-error-publish — THE INLINE HALF, AND A RULING ═══════════════════
	    THE BOOKKEEPING PROBLEM THIS ROUTE EXISTS TO CLOSE. The dashboard has
	    declared `{ state: "error", coverage: "inherits", ref: "T-error-publish" }`
	    since plan 12, and until this route NO SCREEN DESIGNED IT. The contact
	    sheet's ref guard could not catch it, because the guard checks that a ref
	    is a member of CANONICAL_IDS and `T-error-publish` is one — it is a
	    covered-looking cell that was not covered. The gate added in task 3
	    closes the class: an `inherits` ref must now resolve to an artefact some
	    module actually declares, not merely to a legal id.

	    WHY IT IS A SEPARATE ARTEFACT FROM O-publish-invalid. The modal is what
	    the operator sees when they PRESS PUBLISH. This is what the dashboard
	    shows when they arrive and publish is already blocked — the same three
	    failures, no dialog, nothing to close, and the pending list still on the
	    screen underneath. One is an interruption; the other is a condition. The
	    dashboard's `error` cell needs the second, which is why it inherits
	    rather than pointing at the modal.

	    Composed from AlertBanner (279 B under SSR — the component renders) plus
	    the same FormErrorSummary and the same three separated actions, so the
	    two surfaces say the same three things in the same words. */}
	{o === "error-publish" && (
		<div class="ov-treatment" data-artefact="T-error-publish">
			<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">TREATMENT — PUBLISH BLOCKED (D-18)</Eyebrow>
			<AlertBanner
				open
				tone="danger"
				title={INVALID_TITLE}
				description="Nothing has been lost and the pending list below is unchanged. Fix each item on the screen that owns it, then publish. The draft keeps saving while this is on screen — D-18 is lenient about drafts and strict about publishing, and this is the strict end."
			/>
			<FormErrorSummary title={INVALID_TITLE} errors={fx.invalid.errors} />
			<div class="ov-targets" role="group" aria-label="Go to the screen that owns each failure">
				<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">G-6 — SAME MISSING ANCHOR, SECOND SURFACE</Eyebrow>
				<ol class="ov-target-list">
					{fx.invalid.targets.map((t, i) => (
						<li class="ov-target">
							<span class="ov-target-n">{i + 1}</span>
							<Link href={t.href} variant="inline">{t.label}</Link>
						</li>
					))}
				</ol>
			</div>

			<Divider />

			{/* The pending list stays. A blocked publish is not a lost draft. */}
			<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">STILL PENDING, STILL SAVED</Eyebrow>
			<ul class="ov-groups ov-groups-plain">
				{screens.map((s) => (
					<li class="ov-group">
						<div class="ov-group-head">
							<span class="ov-group-entity">{s.entity}</span>
