						</div>
						<ul class="ov-changes">
							{s.changes.map((c) => (
								<li>{c}</li>
							))}
						</ul>
					</li>
				))}
			</ul>
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				{changeCount} changes across {screenCount} screens are still pending. Publishing is what
				is blocked, not editing.
			</Text>
		</div>
	)}

	{/* ══ T-error-network — D-19, RESTATED RATHER THAN RE-CLAIMED ═══════════
	    THE ARTEFACT IS PLAN 15's. `T-error-network` is designed at
	    /admin/projects/cairn/network/, where plan 15 claimed it because
	    UI-SPEC carries ONE contract row for "network / 401" and no screen had a
	    host for it. Re-claiming it here would give one treatment two owners,
	    which is exactly the drift the artefact register exists to prevent — so
	    this route is a CROSS-CHECK, not a second host, and the ARTEFACTS export
	    above deliberately does not list it.

	    WHAT IT IS FOR. D-19 says the expiry is detected ON AUTOSAVE. The modal
	    at plan 15's route is what a long-form editing screen shows; this is the
	    inline band the same detection raises on the publish surface, and the
	    reason it is worth rendering twice is that BOTH MUST SAY THE SAME THING.
	    Two surfaces describing an auth failure differently is how a soft path
	    gets built: one of them eventually offers a way to keep working, and
	    then the admin no longer fails closed. The copy below is byte-identical
	    to plan 15's, and the acceptance grep over this whole file asserts that
	    no alternative route off the surface is offered anywhere in it.

	    NOTHING HERE HOLDS A CREDENTIAL. One action, out to the identity
	    provider. No token, no session value, no form field. */}
	{o === "error-network" && (
		<div class="ov-treatment" data-artefact="T-error-network" data-claimed-at={fx.network.host}>
			<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">TREATMENT — SESSION EXPIRED (D-19)</Eyebrow>
			<AlertBanner open tone="danger" title={NET_TITLE} description={NET_BODY} />
			<div class="ov-actions">
				<Button variant="primary">{NET_CTA}</Button>
			</div>
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				Detected on autosave. The save is retried in place once the session is renewed, so the
				{" "}{changeCount} pending changes below are the ones that get written. They are still
				on this screen and still in the draft; what stopped is the write, and it resumes.
			</Text>

			<Divider />

			<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">THE SAME WORDS, IN TWO PLACES, ON PURPOSE</Eyebrow>
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				The full modal is O-reauth-401, sketched in plan 15 on the longest editing screen in the
				admin. This band is the same treatment where autosave surfaces it. Neither offers any
				action but re-authenticating, and the copy is identical in both so that no later phase
				can pick the softer of two readings.
			</Text>
			{/* THE LINK IS ITS OWN ROW, NOT A WORD IN THE SENTENCE ABOVE. A `Link`
			    written inline measures 16px at every coarse-pointer class — the
			    browser audit caught it here exactly as it caught the identical
			    failure on plan 14's PDF link and plan 15's detail screen. Pulling
			    it out is the fix; claiming WCAG 2.5.8's "in a sentence" exception
			    would be the excuse. */}
			<p class="ov-linkrow">
				<Link href={fx.network.host} variant="inline">O-reauth-401</Link>
			</p>
			<ul class="ov-groups ov-groups-plain">
				{screens.map((s) => (
					<li class="ov-group">
						<div class="ov-group-head">
							<span class="ov-group-entity">{s.entity}</span>
							<span class="ov-group-n">
								{s.changes.length} unsaved {s.changes.length === 1 ? "change" : "changes"}, still
								here
							</span>
						</div>
					</li>
				))}
			</ul>
		</div>
	)}

	{/* ══ O-discard-screen — D-17, THE WEAKER OF TWO GUARDS ═════════════════
	    DS composition per UI-SPEC: `ConfirmDialog tone="danger"`. 0 B under SSR,
	    so modal.css's classes carry the chrome and the danger tone is a local
	    accent on --red-ink. ConfirmDialog's danger tone is a 40px tile in
	    var(--red) over a hardcoded rgba(239,68,68,.1) INSIDE AN INLINE STYLE
	    OBJECT, with no .ds-atom-confirm-panel rule anywhere in dist/css/ —
	    plan 15's F-15-3 — so the tone is unreachable from the charcoal cascade
	    even in a hydrated build.

	    D-11 IS WHAT MAKES THIS COPY TRUE, and it is worth stating because the
	    sentence would otherwise be a promise. A draft is always pending changes
	    LAYERED on published JSON, never an independent fork. So "go back to
	    what's published" is a description of the storage model rather than an
	    undo feature: discarding drops the layer, and the published file
	    underneath was never modified. That is also why the second sentence can
	    say the change cannot be undone without also saying the file is at risk. */}
	{o === "discard-screen" && (
		<div class="ds-atom-modal-backdrop ov-backdrop" data-artefact="O-discard-screen">
			<div
				class="ds-atom-modal ov-panel ov-panel-danger"
				role="alertdialog"
				aria-modal="true"
				aria-labelledby="dsc-title"
				aria-describedby="dsc-desc"
				data-ds-component="ConfirmDialog"
				data-ds-tone="danger"
			>
				<header class="ds-atom-modal-hd">
					<span id="dsc-title" class="ds-atom-modal-hd-title ov-danger">
						{DISCARD_SCREEN_TITLE}
					</span>
				</header>
				<div class="ds-atom-modal-body">
					<p id="dsc-desc" class="ov-lede">{DISCARD_SCREEN_BODY}</p>
					<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">WHAT GOES</Eyebrow>
					<ul class="ov-changes ov-changes-danger">
						{target.changes.map((c) => (
							<li>{c}</li>
						))}
					</ul>
					<p class="ov-note">
						The other {screenCount - 1} screens keep their {changeCount - targetCount} pending
						changes. This is per screen, which is the whole reason it needs a weaker guard than
						the global one.
					</p>
				</div>
				<footer class="ds-atom-modal-ft">
					<Button variant="secondary">{KEEP_EDITING}</Button>
					<Button variant="primary">{DISCARD_SCREEN_CTA}</Button>
				</footer>
			</div>
		</div>
	)}

	{/* ══ O-discard-all — D-17, THE STRONGER GUARD ══════════════════════════
	    DS composition per UI-SPEC: `TypeToConfirm guardWord="discard"`. Measured
	    at 0 B under SSR like the other three, so the panel is modal.css's and
	    the guard field is prototyped — including the property that makes the
	    component what it is: `guardWord` is compared CASE-SENSITIVELY WITH NO
	    TRIM (dist/index.d.ts:1385-1388), so the field below shows the exact word
	    and says so.

	    THE ASYMMETRY IS THE DESIGN. Read the two routes one after the other: one
	    screen is a confirm, everything is a typed word. The guard is proportional
	    to what is destroyed, and the two dialogs are deliberately NOT the same
	    component. A single dialog reused at both scopes would either over-guard
	    the small case until the operator learns to type through it, or
	    under-guard the large one. */}
	{o === "discard-all" && (
		<div class="ds-atom-modal-backdrop ov-backdrop" data-artefact="O-discard-all">
			<div
				class="ds-atom-modal ov-panel ov-panel-danger"
				role="alertdialog"
				aria-modal="true"
				aria-labelledby="dal-title"
				aria-describedby="dal-desc"
				data-ds-component="TypeToConfirm"
				data-ds-guard-word="discard"
			>
				<header class="ds-atom-modal-hd">
					<span id="dal-title" class="ds-atom-modal-hd-title ov-danger">{DISCARD_ALL_TITLE}</span>
				</header>
				<div class="ds-atom-modal-body">
					<p id="dal-desc" class="ov-lede">{DISCARD_ALL_BODY}</p>
					<ul class="ov-groups ov-groups-plain">
						{screens.map((s) => (
							<li class="ov-group">
								<div class="ov-group-head">
									<span class="ov-group-entity">{s.entity}</span>
									<span class="ov-group-n">
